  "plan": {
    "goal": "Recapitulate the very low fluid shear that Caco-2 / intestinal epithelial cells experience under physiological gut flow in an intestinal lumen-on-chip channel.",
    "rationale": "Intestinal epithelial cells experience very low luminal shear under physiological gut flow, far below vascular shear. The literature anchor for intestinal lumen shear is ~0.002-0.02 Pa (0.02-0.2 dyn/cm²); I target 0.005 Pa (0.05 dyn/cm²), the low end of this range, to reproduce the quiescent, low-shear gut lumen. A 1000 µm wide x 100 µm high x 20 mm long PDMS channel at 0.5 µL/min gives 0.005 Pa wall shear (verified via flow_rate_for_shear_stress), Re = 0.015 (deeply laminar), residence time ~240 s/pass, and a negligible 2 Pa pressure drop. Caco-2 is seeded at 100,000 cells/cm² (upper end of the 5e4-1e5 registry range) on the 0.2 cm² channel floor = 20,000 cells/channel. Caco-2 is a barrier line (TEER 250-1000 Ω·cm²); the low shear is chosen to avoid disrupting tight junctions. Medium viscosity taken as water-like 1e-3 Pa·s. Statistical design: 7 biological replicates per group detects a 1.5-SD effect at 80% power (two-sided, alpha 0.05).",
